1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,153
That's the last clause of the CTO's question
from the previous video, and it's a

2
00:00:09,193 --> 00:00:13,611
different kind of question. You've found
a commit, one saved snapshot of the

3
00:00:13,651 --> 00:00:17,365
project. Now you're asked about
sets. Which releases contain it?

4
00:00:17,405 --> 00:00:21,471
Which open branches contain it?
Through which merge did it reach main?

5
00:00:21,511 --> 00:00:25,929
And if somebody amended it away yesterday,
does Git still have the original?

6
00:00:26,269 --> 00:00:31,296
None of these is answered by reading one
commit. Each is a question about membership

7
00:00:31,336 --> 00:00:34,613
in a set of commits, and Git
answers it from the graph.

8
00:00:34,653 --> 00:00:37,328
Hold on to that last
question. It comes back.

9
00:00:39,168 --> 00:00:42,790
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:47,473
In the last video you placed every git
log option in one of four stages: walk,

11
00:00:47,513 --> 00:00:52,517
filter, order, print. Today you use that
model for three set questions, then you add

12
00:00:52,557 --> 00:00:57,440
a tool for a different kind of query, git
grep, which searches content and not the

13
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:02,063
graph, the commits and their parent
links. You finish with a command that old

14
00:01:02,103 --> 00:01:05,365
tutorials still teach and
that Git 2.55 refuses to run.

15
00:01:05,705 --> 00:01:10,416
You're still in scorekit. Keep its graph
in mind: a main branch, a merged side

16
00:01:10,456 --> 00:01:15,350
branch feat/text-utils, and an unmerged
branch feat/report that shares one change

17
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:20,466
with main. A branch is a movable name for
one commit, and a merge joins two lines of

18
00:01:20,506 --> 00:01:20,771
work.

19
00:01:22,611 --> 00:01:26,461
show what each of two branches has that
the other lacks, with copies paired;

20
00:01:26,801 --> 00:01:31,257
find the merge that brought a commit
into a branch with --ancestry-path;

21
00:01:31,597 --> 00:01:34,584
search commits that only
the reflog still reaches;

22
00:01:34,924 --> 00:01:38,433
search tracked content now and in
older revisions with git grep;

23
00:01:38,773 --> 00:01:41,143
replace git whatchanged in an old script.

24
00:01:42,983 --> 00:01:46,664
There are three set
questions in section 14A.14.

25
00:01:47,004 --> 00:01:52,317
Which side has what. You already counted
divergence with --left-right --count, and

26
00:01:52,357 --> 00:01:57,670
you met patch IDs and --cherry-mark in the
videos on cherry-pick, which copies one

27
00:01:57,710 --> 00:02:01,391
commit's change as a new commit.
On screen, the two tips.

28
00:02:01,731 --> 00:02:06,919
The three-dot range main...feat/report is
the commits reachable from either tip and

29
00:02:06,959 --> 00:02:10,888
not from both. Reachable means
found by following parent links.

30
00:02:10,928 --> 00:02:14,416
--left-right marks each one
with the side it belongs to.

31
00:02:14,456 --> 00:02:19,393
--cherry-pick then drops the commits that
have an equivalent on the other side,

32
00:02:19,433 --> 00:02:23,550
equivalent meaning the same patch
ID, a hash of the change itself.

33
00:02:23,590 --> 00:02:25,944
What remains is the work that differs.

34
00:02:26,284 --> 00:02:30,489
Through which commits did a change
arrive. A plain range X..release means

35
00:02:30,529 --> 00:02:35,257
"everything the release has that X does
not have". That includes commits that have

36
00:02:35,297 --> 00:02:38,571
nothing to do with X, because
they were made in parallel.

37
00:02:38,911 --> 00:02:43,487
--ancestry-path keeps only the commits
of the range that are descendants of the

38
00:02:43,527 --> 00:02:47,402
excluded commit. That's the chain
along which the change travelled.

39
00:02:47,442 --> 00:02:51,959
The oldest merge on that chain is the
merge that first carried the change onto

40
00:02:51,999 --> 00:02:52,719
another line.

41
00:02:53,059 --> 00:02:57,936
What only the reflogs know. After an
amend or a rebase, both of which replace

42
00:02:57,976 --> 00:03:02,150
commits by new ones, the old commits
are reachable from no branch.

43
00:03:02,190 --> 00:03:07,514
On screen, main moves to the amended commit,
and the old one, e376e5b, turns dashed.

44
00:03:07,854 --> 00:03:13,798
--all doesn't find them. A reflog is a local
list of the values a branch or HEAD has

45
00:03:13,838 --> 00:03:14,083
had.

46
00:03:14,423 --> 00:03:19,403
--reflog adds every commit mentioned
in any reflog as a starting tip.

47
00:03:19,443 --> 00:03:25,443
It's a stage-one option: it widens the walk,
and any filter, including the pickaxe,

48
00:03:25,483 --> 00:03:30,827
then applies to the wider set. Don't
confuse it with -g, the short form of

49
00:03:30,867 --> 00:03:34,611
--walk-reflogs, which lists
reflog entries in order.

50
00:03:34,651 --> 00:03:37,303
You'll use -g in the recovery module.

51
00:03:37,643 --> 00:03:42,045
Then a second tool. 🟢 SAFE, git grep
searches the content that Git tracks.

52
00:03:42,085 --> 00:03:46,906
Compared with a recursive grep, it skips
.git, ignored build output and untracked

53
00:03:46,946 --> 00:03:51,827
files by default, and it can search any
revision, any commit you can name, without

54
00:03:51,867 --> 00:03:52,788
checking it out.

55
00:03:53,128 --> 00:03:56,594
When do you use which? The
textbook draws the line exactly.

56
00:03:56,634 --> 00:03:59,981
git grep searches states: where
is this text at commit X.

57
00:04:00,021 --> 00:04:04,498
git log -S and -G, the pickaxe, search
transitions: which commit changed it.

58
00:04:04,538 --> 00:04:07,707
To learn when a string came
and went, ask the pickaxe.

59
00:04:07,747 --> 00:04:10,916
Looping git grep over every
revision reads every tree.

60
00:04:11,256 --> 00:04:16,632
When not to trust these answers: --cherry-pick
pairs commits by patch ID, so a copy

61
00:04:16,672 --> 00:04:22,113
that was edited during a conflict isn't
paired. And in a history with several levels

62
00:04:22,153 --> 00:04:27,464
of merges, the oldest merge on the ancestry
path may be a merge into another topic

63
00:04:27,504 --> 00:04:31,901
branch. Then you intersect with
--first-parent of the target branch.

64
00:04:33,741 --> 00:04:39,021
Think of two overlapping circles. The left
circle is everything reachable from main.

65
00:04:39,061 --> 00:04:42,695
The right circle is everything
reachable from feat/report.

66
00:04:42,735 --> 00:04:46,622
The three-dot range is the two
crescents, without the overlap.

67
00:04:46,662 --> 00:04:51,879
--left-right labels the crescents. --cherry-pick
removes pairs that sit in opposite

68
00:04:51,919 --> 00:04:54,159
crescents and carry the same change.

69
00:04:54,499 --> 00:04:59,478
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: draw
the two circles, and mark where a commit

70
00:04:59,518 --> 00:05:03,166
cherry-picked to both branches
belongs. Then say your answer.

71
00:05:08,006 --> 00:05:12,734
Where the picture breaks: the circles suggest
that a commit copied to both branches

72
00:05:12,774 --> 00:05:15,894
is in the overlap. Most people
draw it there. It's not.

73
00:05:15,934 --> 00:05:20,432
A cherry-pick makes a new commit with a
new ID. The two copies sit in different

74
00:05:20,472 --> 00:05:25,200
crescents, and only the patch ID connects
them. That's a computed relationship, not

75
00:05:25,240 --> 00:05:26,061
a recorded one.

76
00:05:26,401 --> 00:05:31,008
For --ancestry-path, change the picture.
A range is a region of the graph.

77
00:05:31,048 --> 00:05:36,157
The ancestry path is the subset of that
region that lies downstream of one commit:

78
00:05:36,197 --> 00:05:41,369
you keep a commit only if you can walk from
it back to the commit you started with.

79
00:05:43,209 --> 00:05:48,791
Read the marks. 2652768 carries
a left angle: only main has it.

80
00:05:48,831 --> 00:05:56,286
a8e8550 and a10f9a1 carry a right angle.
The equals sign was on f37a7d8 and e376e5b:

81
00:05:56,326 --> 00:06:03,514
two commits, two IDs, one patch. With
--cherry-pick, those two disappear from the

82
00:06:03,554 --> 00:06:04,227
listing.

83
00:06:04,567 --> 00:06:11,098
The shaded band is the path. b4b066b,
4da82b5 and 389337a are in the plain range,

84
00:06:11,138 --> 00:06:17,264
because the release has them and dd70d9e
doesn't. They aren't descendants of

85
00:06:17,304 --> 00:06:23,673
dd70d9e, so --ancestry-path drops them.
The first merge on the band is 8657273.

86
00:06:25,513 --> 00:06:30,088
Predict: five commits in the first
listing. How many survive in the second?

87
00:06:30,128 --> 00:06:31,072
Say it out loud.

88
00:06:35,912 --> 00:06:41,966
Three. The README commit exists on both
sides as two commits with the same patch:

89
00:06:42,006 --> 00:06:47,758
e376e5b on the left, f37a7d8 on the
right. With --cherry-pick both disappear.

90
00:06:48,098 --> 00:06:53,333
Now the second question. dd70d9e moved
normalize into its own module, on a side

91
00:06:53,373 --> 00:06:57,005
branch. Is it in the release,
and how did it get there?

92
00:06:57,345 --> 00:07:03,267
Thirteen commits. Point at 389337a, 4da82b5
and b4b066b: they were made on main in

93
00:07:03,307 --> 00:07:08,865
parallel and have nothing to do with the
change. Predict which lines the next

94
00:07:08,905 --> 00:07:10,029
command removes.

95
00:07:10,369 --> 00:07:15,255
Those three are gone. Every commit that
remains has dd70d9e in its ancestry.

96
00:07:15,295 --> 00:07:18,885
Now ask for the merges on that
path and take the oldest.

97
00:07:19,225 --> 00:07:25,117
8657273, "Merge branch 'feat/text-utils'".
In a repository where every change lands

98
00:07:25,157 --> 00:07:30,549
through a merge commit, this is "which
pull request brought this commit in".

99
00:07:30,589 --> 00:07:34,409
A pull request is GitHub's
proposal to merge a branch.

100
00:07:34,749 --> 00:07:39,312
The third question. The script has
amended the newest commit on main.

101
00:07:39,352 --> 00:07:44,848
Quick quiz: does a search with --all still
find the commit that the amend replaced,

102
00:07:44,888 --> 00:07:46,649
yes or no? Say it out loud.

103
00:07:51,489 --> 00:07:55,687
No. --all finds only f37a7d8,
the copy on feat/report.

104
00:07:55,727 --> 00:08:00,708
--reflog also finds e376e5b, the
commit that the amend replaced.

105
00:08:00,748 --> 00:08:07,221
The third command shows why: the reflog of
HEAD still records it at HEAD@{1}, below

106
00:08:07,261 --> 00:08:11,928
the amend at 87f090b. No branch
reaches it. The reflog does.

107
00:08:11,968 --> 00:08:14,675
So yes, Git still has the original.

108
00:08:15,015 --> 00:08:20,998
-n adds line numbers, -c counts matches
per file, -l lists file names only.

109
00:08:21,038 --> 00:08:24,612
No .git, no build output,
no untracked files.

110
00:08:24,952 --> 00:08:30,088
-p shows the function a match belongs to:
the line with the equals signs is the

111
00:08:30,128 --> 00:08:35,526
function header. -W prints that whole
function. For a reviewer, this is the fastest

112
00:08:35,566 --> 00:08:37,491
way to see a match in context.

113
00:08:37,831 --> 00:08:42,629
Now search a revision without checking it
out. Predict the exit status of the second

114
00:08:42,669 --> 00:08:43,090
command.

115
00:08:43,430 --> 00:08:48,624
A revision before the pathspec, the path
pattern, searches that tree, and each hit

116
00:08:48,664 --> 00:08:53,666
is prefixed with the revision. The exit
status is 1 when nothing matches, which

117
00:08:53,706 --> 00:08:55,900
makes git grep -q usable as a test.

118
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:03,101
Several revisions give a quick history of
one line: 0.7 at v0.1.0, 0.75 at v0.2.0.

119
00:09:03,141 --> 00:09:09,496
This tells you the states. It doesn't
tell you which commit made the change.

120
00:09:09,536 --> 00:09:12,693
That's the pickaxe, in the next video.

121
00:09:20,533 --> 00:09:26,088
Exit status 128. Read the first line and
the last: the command is nominated for

122
00:09:26,128 --> 00:09:30,195
removal, and it refuses to run
without --i-still-use-this.

123
00:09:30,235 --> 00:09:32,391
The hint names the replacement.

124
00:09:32,731 --> 00:09:38,982
The same output. Each raw line is old
mode, new mode, old blob ID, new blob ID,

125
00:09:39,022 --> 00:09:45,513
status letter, path. A blob is one file's
bytes. In practice git log --name-status

126
00:09:45,553 --> 00:09:47,981
or git log --stat reads better.

127
00:09:48,321 --> 00:09:54,276
The textbook's sources say the command is
"merely git log with different defaults",

128
00:09:54,316 --> 00:10:00,054
that its manual page carries a deprecation
warning since Git 2.51, and that it's

129
00:10:00,094 --> 00:10:05,831
scheduled for removal in Git 3.0. Write
git log --raw --no-merges, and don't add

130
00:10:05,871 --> 00:10:07,998
--i-still-use-this to scripts.

131
00:10:08,338 --> 00:10:13,647
Going the other way, Git 2.52 added git
last-modified, which reports the commit that

132
00:10:13,687 --> 00:10:15,111
last touched each path.

133
00:10:15,451 --> 00:10:19,623
Its manual page marks it experimental,
so its interface may change.

134
00:10:19,663 --> 00:10:21,697
Don't build a pipeline on it yet.

135
00:10:23,537 --> 00:10:25,185
Five mistakes to watch for.

136
00:10:25,525 --> 00:10:29,016
Reading X..release as "how
X got into the release".

137
00:10:29,056 --> 00:10:34,555
Root cause: a two-dot range is a set
difference, so it includes parallel commits

138
00:10:34,595 --> 00:10:39,885
that are not descendants of X; --ancestry-path
is the restriction you wanted.

139
00:10:40,225 --> 00:10:46,432
Concluding that an amended or rebased commit
is gone because --all does not list it.

140
00:10:46,472 --> 00:10:52,307
Root cause: --all starts from refs, and
the old commit is reachable only from a

141
00:10:52,347 --> 00:10:54,762
reflog; --reflog widens the walk.

142
00:10:55,102 --> 00:11:00,319
Expecting --cherry-pick to pair every
backport. Root cause: the pairing is by patch

143
00:11:00,359 --> 00:11:05,450
ID, which is computed from the change; a
copy whose change differs is a different

144
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:05,830
patch.

145
00:11:06,170 --> 00:11:10,250
Looping git grep over every revision
to find when a string changed.

146
00:11:10,290 --> 00:11:15,107
Root cause: git grep searches states and
reads every tree; the pickaxe searches

147
00:11:15,147 --> 00:11:15,844
transitions.

148
00:11:16,184 --> 00:11:19,557
Keeping git whatchanged alive
with --i-still-use-this.

149
00:11:19,597 --> 00:11:24,740
Root cause: the option silences a removal
notice; it does not change the fact that

150
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:27,078
the command is scheduled for removal.

151
00:11:28,918 --> 00:11:33,676
Now, out of the lab. A retrieval team
ships a model-serving library with release

152
00:11:33,716 --> 00:11:37,933
tags, fixed names for commits. A bug
report names a commit that changed

153
00:11:37,973 --> 00:11:42,971
tokenization. The on-call engineer has to
answer one question for the customer note:

154
00:11:43,011 --> 00:11:47,289
which releases contain that commit,
and through which merge did it land?

155
00:11:47,629 --> 00:11:52,521
She runs the ancestry-path query from the
commit to main, restricted to merges, and

156
00:11:52,561 --> 00:11:56,741
takes the oldest. That names the
merge, and therefore the pull request.

157
00:11:56,781 --> 00:12:01,079
Then she checks an older maintenance
branch with a left-right listing and

158
00:12:01,119 --> 00:12:06,071
--cherry-pick, because fixes are backported
there by cherry-pick and the IDs differ.

159
00:12:06,111 --> 00:12:10,945
The note says which query produced each
statement. Nobody had to remember what was

160
00:12:10,985 --> 00:12:11,658
merged when.

161
00:12:13,498 --> 00:12:20,908
Your turn. Do Exercise 11.4, Level 2,
command prediction, "Ranges on a diverged

162
00:12:20,948 --> 00:12:26,189
branch", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

163
00:12:26,529 --> 00:12:31,275
It's a prediction exercise, so the rule is
strict: for each command write down the

164
00:12:31,315 --> 00:12:34,836
number of lines and the markers
you expect before you run it.

165
00:12:34,876 --> 00:12:39,680
Draw the two crescents first. Then run the
commands in labs/shell and explain every

166
00:12:39,720 --> 00:12:42,598
difference between your
prediction and the output.

167
00:12:42,938 --> 00:12:48,565
The challenge is Exercise 11.8, Level 3,
"Git lost my commit", in the same file.

168
00:12:50,405 --> 00:12:51,682
Pause and answer out loud.

169
00:12:56,522 --> 00:13:01,832
A strong answer treats this as a reachability
question and says so in the first

170
00:13:01,872 --> 00:13:07,385
sentence. It separates tags from branches,
and local branches from remote-tracking

171
00:13:07,425 --> 00:13:12,938
ones. It says what the answer misses: a
copy of the commit made by cherry-pick has

172
00:13:12,978 --> 00:13:18,423
another ID, so containment by ID isn't the
whole story, and it names the patch-ID

173
00:13:18,463 --> 00:13:23,638
tool that covers the gap. It can also
say how the commit travelled, using the

174
00:13:23,678 --> 00:13:29,326
ancestry path and the oldest merge on it,
and what to do when the merges are nested.

175
00:13:31,166 --> 00:13:34,093
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

176
00:13:34,433 --> 00:13:41,631
A three-dot range with --left-right shows
what each side has alone; --cherry-pick

177
00:13:41,671 --> 00:13:44,670
removes pairs with the same patch.

178
00:13:45,010 --> 00:13:49,999
--ancestry-path reduces a range to the
descendants of the excluded commit, and the

179
00:13:50,039 --> 00:13:53,617
oldest merge on that path is
where the change first landed.

180
00:13:53,957 --> 00:14:00,166
--reflog widens the walk to commits that
no ref reaches; -g lists reflog entries.

181
00:14:00,506 --> 00:14:04,855
git grep searches states, at any revision;
the pickaxe searches transitions.

182
00:14:05,195 --> 00:14:11,687
git whatchanged refuses to run on Git
2.55; its replacement is git log --raw

183
00:14:11,727 --> 00:14:12,718
--no-merges.

184
00:14:14,558 --> 00:14:22,027
Read sections 14A.14, 14A.23
and 14A.24 of Chapter 14A.

185
00:14:22,367 --> 00:14:26,954
Today you asked Git about sets of commits,
and you found a commit that no branch

186
00:14:26,994 --> 00:14:30,598
reaches. Draw the two crescents
on paper before the next video.

187
00:14:30,638 --> 00:14:35,225
Next time: the history of one file. Until
then, look at the state first and type

188
00:14:35,265 --> 00:14:37,076
second. See you in the next one.
